-## Subtopics
-
-### Depth-Plane Reduction
-A related technique that reduces the number of depth planes by shifting voxels forward to fill empty space, reducing high frequencies [S1]. It is chosen when the volume surface is sparse compared to a solid object.
-
-### Luminance Preservation
-While chroma is subsampled, luminance (Y) is kept at full resolution to maintain structural detail and brightness accuracy [S1].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Low Bitrate Loss:** Without chroma subsampling, chroma information can be completely lost at low bitrates, resulting in grey objects [S1].
+*   **Outliers:** Using the mean instead of the median would make the result more sensitive to outlier color values in sparse regions [S1].
+*   **Transparency:** Correctly identifying "no existing voxel" is critical; treating empty space as a zero-color value instead of transparent would introduce artifacts [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Compression Ratio:** Achieves significant bitrate reduction for color components. In the Leo sequence experiment, chroma subsampling achieved 39.30 dB PSNR at 1.18 bpv (bits per voxel), whereas non-subsampling required 2.90 bpv for the same quality [S1].
-*   **Quality:** Prevents the "grey object" artifact seen in non-subsampled low-bitrate volumes [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Better sharpness than average-based subsampling; less influenced by outliers; preserves color quality at low bitrates [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires handling of missing values on the decoder side; relies on the assumption that viewers' color perception is robust to the repeating interpolation strategy [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When compressing multiview or volumetric data where color fidelity is important but bitrate is constrained, and the volume is sparse (surface-based) rather than solid [S1].
+*   **Compression Ratio:** In the Leo sequence experiment, chroma subsampling achieved 39.30 dB PSNR at 1.18 bits per voxel (bpv), whereas the non-subsampling approach required 2.90 bpv for the same quality [S1].
+*   **Quality:** Subjective results show that at 0.2 bpv, non-subsampled objects appear grey, while subsampled objects retain color [S1].
+*   **Complexity:** The encoding complexity is linear with respect to the number of voxels, dominated by the median calculation per group. Decoding involves simple lookup and repetition.
+*   **Strengths:** Preserves color quality at very low bitrates; robust against outliers; simple to implement.
+*   **Limitations:** Only applies to chrominance; requires YUV color space separation; assumes a regular grid structure for grouping.
 
 ## Background
-The method was proposed in the context of color volumetric compression for realistic view synthesis applications [S1]. It addresses the limitations of direct subsampling in volumetric data, where intensity and color are defined only for existing voxels, making standard image subsampling techniques ineffective [S1]. The approach was tested on multiview data captured at the University of Bristol [S1].
+Chroma subsampling for volumetric data was proposed in the context of realistic view synthesis applications, specifically for multiview video and volume compression [S1]. The method was tested on the multiview Leo sequence captured at the University of Bristol [S1]. It addresses the specific challenge that in volumes, intensity and color are defined only for existing voxels, making direct subsampling ineffective compared to image-based approaches [S1].
 
